The printer writes as print.c's printchar and strout do

GNU's printer writes while it walks.  print.c's printchar hands one character to the output and strout a block: into print_buffer for a buffer or a marker, which print_finish inserts; onto stdout in a batch session, one fwrite a character and one a block; into the echo area and the message log in an interactive one; or to a function, called with each character.  Emaxx's printer built an object's whole text and handed it over when the walk was done.  So Lisp the printer runs (`print-unreadable-function', a function it prints to) found nothing written where GNU has written everything before it, an error in the middle of a print lost the text GNU had already put out, stdout was written in other pieces than GNU's, and after a failed write of stdout nothing was left for exit's report to fail on (round 363's second review, recorded then).

The output now remembers how it was written (print/output.rs): which bytes a strout wrote as one block, which of those are a C string's octets, that strout was called at all, and every other character as a printchar's.  PrintSink (print.rs) is printcharfun as print_prepare leaves it, and `deliver' is printchar and strout for each piece.  The walk (print/object.rs) writes where print.c writes.  For a buffer or a marker it writes into print_buffer itself, the one the interpreter holds as print.c holds its static, and gives it back before any Lisp runs.  For a function every character is a call where the walk stands, nothing kept.  For stdout it keeps up to a stdio buffer's worth, since the stream makes the same writes of the same fwrites whenever they come, and hands that over before Lisp runs, when the walk ends, and when it stops on an error (what it wrote first, so an error of the output's own comes first).  file_io.rs's with_print_stream is print_prepare and print_finish around a body that is given the sink: Fprin1, Fprinc and Fprint (its two newlines printchars), Fterpri, Fwrite_char and write_string each run inside one.

On stdout that gives GNU's writes.  `(prin1 (make-string 20000 ?x))' to a pipe leaves a buffer at a time, where `princ' of it is the buffer and then its whole blocks in one write; on a terminal a newline printchar wrote goes out at once and a strout's block through its last newline; and after a write has failed the buffer fills again, so close_output_streams' report names the device's error where it said Success.  `standard-display-table' shows what goes to stdout (printchar_to_stream, with stderr's from round 363), one character at a time, so a character its coding system signals for owes the session no newline.  `terpri' with ENSURE asks for the last character printchar wrote (`printchar_stdout_last'), which a guess per kind of object answered before; for that the walk's pieces had to be print.c's everywhere: a fixnum's digits one strout, nil and t printchars as the symbols they are, an octal escape printchars, `#<subr NAME>' two print_c_strings and a printchar, a frame's, window's, process's, thread's, mutex's and condition variable's name through print_string, a bool vector's bytes as print_bool_vector writes them.  A strout of nothing still owes the batch session its newline before the next message (`noninteractive_need_newline'), so `(princ "")' does.

A function printed to is called where the walk stands, and what it changes is what the printer reads next.  Each print variable is read where the printer first needs it and again once Lisp has run (after `print-unreadable-function' too, where only the escape variables were read again).  What print.c writes before it reads is written before: a list's parenthesis before its first element and `print-length', a quote before the quoted object, `#<buffer ' before the buffer's name, a label's `#1=' before its table entry is rewritten, a hash table's `#s(hash-table' before its size, test and entries, a string's text before its intervals.  A symbol's name is read for each character, `princ' reads its string again for each character with the sizes it read at the start (a string the function makes shorter is read at its terminating NUL), and `prin1' reads the escape variables for each character of the copy print_prune_string_charset made, or of the string itself when `print-charset-text-property' is t.  A string found in `print-number-table' is a strout of its data as it is, a unibyte one's bytes handed to a function as the C chars they are.

print.c's statics are shared by every print, and Emaxx's now are.  The print stack is one (PRSTACK, as round 348's read stack): each print works above the depth it found and nothing takes entries off when a print is left by a throw or an error, so the print it was run from goes on with them, and GNU's `(a b z) c)' for a list whose function prints `(x y z)' and throws at its `y' is Emaxx's.  print_buffer is one: a print into a buffer run from inside another sets the outer text aside, and print_unwind copies it back but leaves `pos' and `pos_byte' where the inner print ended, so the outer print goes on from there: `(prin1-to-string (list 1 2 (current-buffer) 3))' with a function that calls `prin1-to-string' of `zz' is "(1zz 3)" in both.  Fprinc's fast path is print_string alone for a string with no intervals when `print-continuous-numbering' is nil, so such a `princ' inside a print leaves its depth and numbering alone; `print-level' is compared as any fixnum, so under a negative one every cons is "...".

print_finish is print.c's.  It runs before print_buffer is given back, in the buffer current then: insert_1_both of the text with no properties inherited and no update_compositions, then `signal_after_change (PT - print_buffer.pos, 0, print_buffer.pos)'.  insert_1_both copies out of print_buffer after prepare_to_modify_buffer, so a print a before-change hook runs into a buffer (`format' with %S is one) writes over the waiting text and leaves `pos' where it ended: the after-change hooks are told of that many characters (GNU's `(9 11 0)' for ten inserted at 1, `(-26 3 0)' when the hook printed more than was waiting), and after two such prints the text inserted is what the two partial copies left ("ouz1r-text" for `outer-text').  A print of no characters is no insertion (a read-only buffer takes it) and still the signal.  The marker printed to is set in the buffer current after the insertion, where its hooks or a destination killed during the walk left the print; and print_prepare's record_unwind_current_buffer makes the buffer the print began in current again whatever a function printed to or `print-unreadable-function' selected.  A marker outside its buffer's accessible part is the error's datum.

Fwrite_char and Fexternal_debugging_output check for a fixnum and no more, and printchar and printchar_to_stream take it as an unsigned int.  A function is called with that number; any other output stores CHAR_STRING's bytes: char_string resolves a modifier bit and drops the rest, and signals for a code still past MAX_CHAR, where Emaxx panicked on some and wrote others; a negative int is its low byte alone in printchar and the two-byte form's arithmetic in printchar_to_stream, as the oracle's build makes of an int `eassume (0 <= c)' does not hold for.

print_error_message is one function over any stream (display.rs): Ferror_message_string gives it the hidden prin1 buffer, and Fcommand_error_default_function `external-debugging-output' and Fterpri in a batch session, t in an interactive one, where it printed Ferror_message_string's text.  A batch error's message and data therefore reach stderr as `princ' and `prin1' hand them to that function: a unibyte string's bytes as the characters of those codes from `princ', as raw bytes from `prin1', and no `print-escape-nonascii' as the buffer bound; the context is write_string of the C string it is (a function handed each byte as a C char, the echo area a character a byte and the log the text); a caller that is t is logged as a symbol is.  In an interactive session print_prepare's setup_echo_area_for_printing runs for a print to t before anything is printed; `terpri' with ENSURE asks for the beginning of a line in the echo area's text, and `current-message' is nil while that text is empty.

The printer's stream is the variable's dynamic value.  print.c reads Vstandard_output and its flag `noninteractive' as C variables, and Emaxx read both through the lexical environment its primitives are handed, so `(eval '(princ "x") '((standard-output . ignore)))' printed nothing where GNU prints to stdout; printer_stream_value, PrintSink::of, Fterpri's batch branch and readevalloop's test now read the dynamic values.  (The same holds for 523 other reads in the primitives, recorded for a round of their own.)  And `prin1' prints the copy print_prune_string_charset makes of a string whatever it prints to, so a string is consed for each as in GNU: `memory-use-counts' agrees for a function, a buffer, a string and stdout, and a loop of prints collects as often as GNU's.

What Lisp does while a print runs is read where print.c reads it, and what the print reads stays alive.  The string `prin1' goes on with is print_prune_string_charset's copy, a Lisp string of its own made before `#(' is written, and its intervals are read from it one at a time; the first draft of this round held them in a host vector the collector does not scan, and a function printed to that dropped the string's properties and collected got freed conses printed.  print_preprocess's work list and a tree-sitter record's fields are scanned for the same reason.  A marker's and an overlay's buffer are read again for the name once the positions are written, a window's buffer after " on ", a frame's name before `#<frame ', a font spec's slots each at its turn; print_error_message reads a datum once its separator is written and the tail once the datum is printed (FOR_EACH_TAIL).  The print stack's entries go when no print_object is running: print.c's stack is no root of its collector, and a scanned one that nothing emptied kept every object of a print left by a throw alive for the session (2.4 GB after 3,000 such prints of a large vector, 59 MB in GNU).  `standard-display-table' is looked up as disptab.h does (CharTableRef::is_display_table and disp_char_vector: an ASCII character from the table's own ASCII slot and never its parent, the default only while no ASCII character has an entry, six extra slots required), terpri's error for a function is signal_error's, whose proper list is the data's tail, and a label whose negation is no fixnum wraps as make_fixnum does.

Tests.  Library: a_function_stream_is_called_as_the_printer_walks, prints_into_buffers_share_print_buffer and a_print_goes_on_from_what_lisp_left_it are pinned against GNU; the stream's characters_fill_the_buffer_where_a_block_is_written_whole and a_run_of_characters_is_written_as_each_character_is.  cli_parity runs GNU and Emaxx side by side in printed_text_reaches_its_output_as_the_walk_goes, a_print_into_a_buffer_inside_another_shares_the_print_buffer, terpri_asks_what_printchar_last_wrote (42 kinds of print), the_standard_display_table_shows_what_goes_to_stdout, a_batch_error_is_printed_onto_the_debugging_output, a_print_of_nothing_still_owes_the_batch_newline, printing_to_t_fills_the_echo_area_and_the_message_log (a terminal session), a_print_copies_its_text_once_the_change_hooks_have_run, a_code_that_is_no_character_is_written_as_char_string_stores_it, a_print_ends_in_the_buffer_it_began_in, the_printer_reads_its_variables_as_c_reads_them, prin1_conses_the_copy_print_prune_string_charset_makes, what_lisp_changes_during_a_print_is_read_where_print_c_reads_it, a_print_keeps_what_it_reads_alive_and_no_longer, the_standard_display_table_is_looked_up_as_disp_char_vector_does and stdout_is_written_a_character_and_a_block_at_a_time, which gives each editor a datagram socket for stdout and compares the writes one by one.

Beyond the tests, PROBES364

REVIEW364

HANDOVER records round 364, the CI of the round 363 head and the findings above.
